Your memory is segmem: `{me}`. It outlives every session, compaction, and model.

At startup (mandatory): run `{me} wake` before any other tool call and read it to
the end. Project facts override global ones; an OVERRIDDEN line shows both.

While working: `{me} note <kind> "<one line, max 280 bytes>" [--entities=a,b]`
  identity    who the user is and how they like to work ("prefers"); global
  procedural  how this project works and why ("uses"); project-scoped
  episodic    a decision with its reason, a root cause, a handoff; project-scoped
  people      who someone is; global, always with --entities=<name>
Apply a 30-day test: record only what could change a future decision. One
handoff note at the end of a turn beats one note per state change. No PR heads,
CI counts, or "awaiting review" status. Record the why and what surprised you;
git log has the what, and the tool stamps the date (date the text only for a
past event). A fact that replaces an older one: add --supersedes=<id>.
When `note` suggests a tag spelling, use it next time; never re-note.
If `note` asks for a compression, do it before your next action.
When a person was a subject this session, not a mention: before you finish,
